HAND-WRITTEN, NOT RECORDED.

lsp-languages.json is what `kiln lsp --languages --format json` should
print for a project whose [code] paths hold Rust and Tcl files. It was
written by hand from Kiln's unreleased, unverified commit 4592faf ("Lsp:
kiln lsp --languages says what init --fenix would register", never
compiled): its test `lsp_languages_lists_what_init_fenix_would_register`
expects exactly these keys and values, and kiln's `json_out` prints keys
in name order, two spaces deep, as its other answers in ../out/ are.

No released kiln prints it yet (kiln 1.0.0 refuses --languages; its real
refusal is recorded in ../out/demo/lsp-languages.*). Once a kiln that has
it is released, record.sh records the real answer in ../out/, and the
tests should read that instead of this file.

---

export-*: what `kiln export` prints that can't be recorded without a
Confluence server (record.sh never reaches one) or with the Secret
Service this recording machine lacks. Each is `<name>.json` or
`<name>.stderr` with `<name>.exit`, as record.sh writes them. Written from
Kiln's source at 0cc4568 (crates/kiln/src/cmd/export.rs, unchanged at
4592faf), and from the recorded answers in ../out/exports/ for everything
else in them (the plan, the commit, the sizes):

- export-publish-applied.json, exit 0: `kiln export
  design-doc-confluence --yes --format json` after a successful publish
  of the plan in ../out/exports/confluence-update.json. JSON output
  publishes before printing (export.rs:312-328) and `applied` is
  `applied_json` (export.rs:454-458, 789-794): created, updated, retired,
  pruned, skipped. Exit 0 (export.rs:382).
- export-publish-applied-other-plan.json, exit 0: the same, when the
  plan kiln made as it published (it reads the space again: it never
  publishes a dry run's plan) differs from the one the dry run showed --
  here the old section had been retired by someone else meanwhile, so
  kiln's plan updates the page and retires nothing.
- export-publish-nothing.json, exit 0: kiln's own plan, read as it
  published, has nothing to do: `publish` returns before writing
  (export.rs:683-685), `applied` stays null, exit 0. The op is what the
  planner gives an unchanged page (crates/kiln-confluence/src/plan.rs:
  `Unchanged`, no reasons, message "published", plan.rs:334-347).
- export-publish-refused.json, exit 1: the plan kiln read as it published
  is refused (the page was edited in Confluence since the dry run):
  nothing is published (export.rs:679-682), the JSON with the refusal is
  printed and the exit is 1 (export.rs:379-381). The JSON is the recorded
  dry run's, ../out/exports/confluence-drift.json, which is what the
  same snapshot plans.
- export-publish-network.stderr, exit 3: a publish stopped half-way by
  the server or the network (export.rs:761-766: "publishing `NAME`
  stopped: {e}; run again to finish", EXIT_NETWORK). The `{e}` is shaped
  as the recorded read error in ../out/exports/confluence-unreachable.stderr
  is, for a PUT.
- export-publish-space-changed.stderr, exit 1: a publish whose snapshot
  is over two minutes old and whose fresh plan differs (export.rs:711-729).
- export-no-token.stderr, export-publish-no-token.stderr, exit 2: no
  token, where the credential store answers that it has none (a desktop
  with a Secret Service, Windows): export.rs:570-583, with " (or plan
  offline with --snapshot FILE)" only for --dry-run and --check. What a
  machine without a Secret Service prints instead is recorded in
  ../out/exports/confluence-no-token.stderr.

---

comments*, jira-*: what `kiln confluence comments`, `kiln list --jira`,
`kiln jira sync` and `kiln jira pull` print that can't be recorded without
a Confluence or Jira server (record.sh never reaches one: its comments
and Jira runs are the refusals, the connection refused at 127.0.0.1:9, and
`list --jira` over a mirror cache it writes, all in ../out/comments/ and
../out/jira/). Written from Kiln's source at 0cc4568 (unchanged at
4592faf); the tickets, people and pages in them are made up. Each is
`<name>.json` or `<name>.out` (stdout) and `<name>.stderr`, with
`<name>.exit`, as record.sh writes them:

- comments.json, exit 0: `kiln confluence comments --format json` for the
  two Confluence targets of ../out/exports/kiln.toml. An array of
  `{comments, name, space}` (crates/kiln/src/cmd/confluence.rs:33-38; no
  `kiln_json`, which json_out puts only into objects, cmd/mod.rs:54-61),
  keys in name order as every kiln JSON here is. Each comment is
  kiln-confluence's `Placed` (crates/kiln-confluence/src/comments.rs:
  22-31, built at 61-76): `item` is the item it is about or null, `text`
  one line (kiln's own `one_line`, comments.rs:202-211), `url` made from
  `[confluence] url` and the page and comment ids (comments.rs:73-75),
  `created` as Confluence gives it (its fake: 2026-10-12T09:00:00.000Z,
  crates/kiln-confluence/src/fake.rs:283).
- comments-unpublished.json, exit 0: a target with no page yet is left out
  of the JSON (confluence.rs:35, `.filter(|t| t.published)`); the text
  form says "not published yet" (confluence.rs:43-45).
- comments-hostile.json, exit 0: NOT what kiln prints: a comment whose
  author and text hold an escape sequence, a control character, markup and
  a `javascript:` link, to test that the tab shows other people's writing
  as plain text on one line and never opens a link that isn't http(s).
  kiln builds `url` from `[confluence] url` and numbers, so it can't print
  that link; the page doesn't rely on it.
- comments-no-token.stderr, exit 2: no token, where the credential store
  answers that it has none (a desktop with a Secret Service, Windows):
  confluence.rs:103-108. What a machine without one prints is recorded in
  ../out/comments/no-token.stderr. Every failure of `confluence comments`
  fails the whole command (the `?` at confluence.rs:129 and 146, exit 3 for
  the server): there is no per-target error, and so none in the tab.
- jira-sync-dry-run*.out: `kiln jira sync --dry-run`, exit 0, or 1 when a
  ticket is refused or the plan is (jira.rs:287-288). One line per
  operation, as `plan_lines` writes them (jira.rs:167-247): the heading
  (174-178), `create`, `update`, `move`, `refused`, `orphan`, `left alone`
  (184-224), the totals (233-240) and, after them, one `  refused: why` per
  plan-level refusal (241-243). The names are the demo's. No `--format`:
  `jira sync` has none (crates/kiln/src/main.rs, `JiraCommand::Sync`).
  -first is the first sync (no `parent` under `[jira]` yet, 176-185),
  -nothing a plan with nothing to do, -plan-refused a plan-level refusal
  (crates/kiln-jira/src/sync.rs:224-228).
- jira-sync-applied*.out: `kiln jira sync --yes`: the plan as printed
  (jira.rs:283-285), then the summary (jira.rs:332-345): created, updated
  and moved, and `; N file(s) gained ticket keys (commit them)` when keys
  were written; then `  refused: …` for what Jira refused as it applied
  (346-348). Exit 0, or 1 with refusals or keys that couldn't be written
  (358-361). -no-keys is a sync that created nothing, so no keys.
- jira-sync-partial.out/.stderr, exit 3: Jira failed part way. The keys of
  what was created are written first and the summary printed, then the
  error, `…; run it again to finish` (jira.rs:314-357, push.rs:152 for
  `Jira (URL): …`). The `{e}` after it is shaped as the recorded read
  error in ../out/jira/sync-unreachable.stderr is, for a POST.
- jira-sync-write-error.out/.stderr, exit 1: the keys couldn't be written
  into an item (a file changed on disk), jira.rs:318-330 and 349-360.
- jira-sync-no-token.stderr (exit 2), jira-list-no-token.stderr (exit 3):
  no token on a desktop with a credential store (jira.rs:143-150 for
  sync and pull, 60-65 for the mirror). Note the exit codes: the mirror's
  every failure is exit 3 (cmd/list.rs:94, EXIT_NETWORK), sync's and
  pull's token errors exit 2 (`Fail::usage`). What this machine prints is
  recorded in ../out/jira/*-no-token.*.
- jira-pull.out, jira-pull-nothing.out, jira-pull-no-ticket.stderr,
  jira-pull-no-mapping.stderr: `kiln jira pull -- ID` (jira.rs:373-433):
  what `kiln set` prints (`TASK-002: status doing → done`, edit.rs:266 of
  kiln-index) and ` (from DEMO-102 Done)` (426); "every item's status
  already matches its ticket" (431); exit 1 with a stderr line for an item
  with no ticket under the project issue (399-406) or a Jira status that
  maps to no status (411-417).
